{
  "dom_id": "step-q72337",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z-4=(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}$ and the plane $x+y=-4$ onto $yz$-plane.",
  "worked_solution": "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\nx+y=-4\\,⟹\\,x=-4-y\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-4 & =((-4-y)-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} \\\\ z-4 & =(-y-6)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} \\\\ z-4 & ={y}^{2}+12y+36+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} \\\\ z-4 & =\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}+12x+36 \\\\ z-4 & =\\frac{3}{2}({y}^{2}+8y+24) \\\\ z-4 & =\\frac{3}{2}(({y}^{2}+8y+16)+8) \\\\ z-4 & =\\frac{3}{2}(y+4)^{2}+12 \\\\ z & =\\frac{3}{2}(y+4)^{2}+16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the parabola $z=\\frac{3}{2}(y+4)^{2}+16.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
